Dev team member Ken Arromdee writes:
>How about a "water confirm" option?  (It would work _whenever_ you tried to
>move onto a pool, so if you did want to go on the pool (i.e. were levitating)
>you'd have to turn it off or else have to answer yes each move).

A good suggestion!  Hopefully the default for this option will be ON.
(beware implicit spoilers in discussion below)

In an ideal world, you wouldn't be asked to confirm if flying or
levitating since there is obviously no risk then.  If the player
doesn't know it is safe to go over the water then, it is unlikely that
he will think of trying, so little is given away.  *water walking*
boots are another matter, since their effect is not obvious when on
land.  I don't know if these checks are worth coding in.

I don't know if you guys will every add large bodies of water (the one
in the stronghold is not so bad), but imagine confirming every move in
the middle of an ocean.  Confirming to stand still over a pool once
there seems even sillier.  Perhaps you could check whether the player
is *already* over water before asking for a second confirmation.  This
sounds very codable.

(a spoiler really follows)

Do you know what happens when you are a vampire lord, flying over
water, and then you polymorph into something that can't fly?  Nothing.
Now that seems like a more realistic way to drown than pressing the
wrong key.  I'm not begging for a fix, though, unless the water is
made somewhat survivable per Hairston's question.  Speaking of which,
if you stripped naked, should you be able to dive back in for stuff
lost in the water?

